## 2026-11-11: watchdog stopped on one empty listing after DONE (harness3, scenario 5)
Mistake: before the hard deadline, the watchdog exited on DONE plus one empty listing; a delayed creation behind an ambiguous answer appeared later with no supervision. My first regression test also passed before the fix, because its assertion held when the watchdog quit early.
Guard: `test_r11_watchdog_before_the_deadline_settles_so_a_delayed_creation_is_deleted` (seen RED, then GREEN) and the harness scenario "delayed creation behind an ambiguous answer". Rule: a regression test must assert that the triggering event happened, not only that the bad state is absent.

## 2026-11-11: churn memory preflight measured reused records; worker limit reset per command (churn review r4)
Mistake: the pod-side preflight took peak memory from a child whose task was "skipped" (a valid record existed), so it could admit too many workers; the reduced worker limit lived only inside one `_run_rate` call, so a separate diagnostics command ran at nine workers. The smoke test itself asserted on a skipped measurement and passed.
Guard: the child reports the task status and `memory_preflight` refuses skipped measurements; `memory_workers.json` keeps the lowest limit per memory limit; tests `test_r4_*` (5, seen RED) and the smoke test now assert the refusal on an existing record and a real child run. Rule: a resource measurement must prove the measured work ran (status, record written), not only that a number came back.

## 2026-11-11: churn r5, a reuse path trusted records it could not verify
Mistake: the r4 fix reused a saved memory measurement without checking rule coverage or that draft-4 records carried real-run evidence, and it removed measured jobs from dispatch, so a missing record would stay missing.
Guard: versioned preflight record with per-task "done" statuses and rule coverage; every job goes through `_run_one`; tests `test_r5_*` (4, seen RED). Rule: a cache or reuse path must validate the same things the fresh path proves, and must never stand in for the production record check.

## 2026-11-11: launcher review r11 (reset pod)
Mistake: (a) the watchdog counted empty time before DONE existed, so it could exit with zero settle after DONE; (b) every HTTP error counted as a definite refusal, but a 5xx or gateway error can follow a real creation; (c) `podctl.py reconcile NAME 600` passed 600 as KEEP_ID, not as a timeout; (d) retrying after an ambiguous answer could overlap two pods, which no listing can rule out.
Guard: tests `test_r12_settle_counts_only_after_done`, `test_r12_creation_answers_are_ambiguous_unless_clearly_refused` (10 cases), `test_r12_after_an_ambiguous_answer_the_settle_is_long_enough_for_a_late_pod`, `test_r12_fractional_budget_is_passed_as_a_fractional_timeout` (all seen RED). Design guard for (d): an ambiguous answer ends creation for the launch. Rule: prefer a design that removes an unprovable case over code that tries to prove it.

- 2026-11-11 (brief r12): I described the harness late-pod delay as "20 s"; the value is a countdown of 20 fake API calls (more than 300 s). Guard: copy a harness parameter into a brief only with its unit as the harness comment states it (`test_reset_pod_harness.sh` line 21), and quote the scenario title.
- 2026-11-11 (brief r12): I labelled harness log times (local, UTC+1) as UTC. Guard: take every time in a brief or on the board from `date -u` in its own call; never relabel a log time.
- 2026-11-11 (launcher r12-r13): the price fix went into the watchdog only; the reviewers then found the cleanup reconcile, and next the interrupted-cleanup path, one round each. Guard: when a review names one path of a rule (here "record the price before any deletion"), list every code path of that kind with `grep -n delete reset_pod.sh podctl.py` and fix and test all of them in the same round; state the list in the brief.
- 2026-11-11 (harness 9): I started the harness with a trailing `&` in a foreground call, so it was untracked; I stopped it by its exact command line and restarted it with run_in_background. Guard: long local runs start only with run_in_background, never with `&`.
- 2026-11-11 (launcher r12-r15): four review rounds each found new gaps in a self-made spend bound (rate file, unknown-price marker, lock). Guard: when a review loop keeps finding defects in one mechanism, replace the mechanism with an external record before patching again (r16: the account balance difference); ask "can this part be removed?" at the second rejection on the same mechanism.
- 2026-11-11 (harness 12): a macOS update restarted the Mac mid-run; the harness and its log in the /tmp scratchpad were lost (about 2 h). Guard: write long-run logs under results/pods/logs_harness/ (persists across restarts), and after any restart first check `runpod.py list` and `podctl.py balance` before relaunching.
- 2026-11-11 (harness 13): the harness ran under run_in_background, so it died with the Claude session when the session restarted (5 of 14 scenarios done, about 1.7 h lost). This replaces the harness-9 guard. Guard: long local runs start detached (`nohup ... < /dev/null &`, `disown`), write a pid file and the file hashes at the top of a log under results/pods/logs_harness/, and a separate tracked waiter (run_in_background) watches the pid and the log; after a session restart, check the pid before anything else.
- 2026-11-11 (reset results r1): I started the two reviewers with `set -- $seat` in the session shell, which is zsh; zsh does not split the words, so codex got no model and both seats failed at once. Guard: reviewers start only through `results/pods/review_start.sh TOPIC` (a bash script that refuses a missing brief or an existing review file).
- 2026-11-11 (spend wait): a wait script did integer arithmetic on the decimal timestamp in `NAME.wdend`, failed, and asked for the spend record early; `podctl.py spend` refused it, as designed. Guard: the existing refusal (no new rule); take the integer part (`cut -d. -f1`) when a shell script reads `wdend`.

## 2026-11-11: a response note claimed wording the paper does not have

- **Mistake:** `RESPONSE.md` for the Fable round-3 package said the priority-phase text uses "inside search error". The paper has no such words; Fable found the gap. The package's ledger copy also had no reset entry, so the plan-before-results timing was not checkable.
- **Guard:** before a review package is sent, every quoted or paraphrased paper claim in its response note is checked with `grep` on main.tex, and the package includes the commits that prove timing (plan freeze, analysis code, pod gate record).
- **Guard, strengthened (same day):** `docs/revision/check_response_quotes.py` (tests in `test_check_response_quotes.py`, including the round-3 phrase as the negative case) must print "quotes ok" on a response note before its package is zipped. A response note shows each claimed paper change as a quotation, so that the check covers it. Phrases quoted only as removed wording are listed by the check and confirmed by hand.

## 2026-11-11: two mistakes found by external review r12

- **Mistake 1:** I adopted Fable r3's "nesting failure" reading of a negative held-out I, and its rule that the doubled value is "more reliable", although the paper's own X7-SC caveat says held-out order need not follow nesting. Sol and Astra did not catch it either.
- **Guard 1:** every internal-review brief for text written in response to a reviewer now asks: "Does any adopted reviewer inference contradict a qualification the paper already states? Quote both." (Written rule; the brief template is `docs/revision/roster/brief-*.md`.)
- **Mistake 2:** the round-12 response note said every change was text or analysis on existing records, but the reference check ran new Mac evaluations.
- **Guard 2:** a response note classifies each change as text, analysis on existing records, or new evaluation; `check_response_quotes.py` does not cover this, so the brief for the package's last internal check asks for it explicitly.
- 2026-11-11: paper_commit.sh committed without the regenerated numbers.tex (54ee50c). Guard: the script now copies $S/numbers.tex into paper-v2 before git add.
- 2026-11-12: the churn launcher's end state kept a trailing space (the polled log tail is joined with `tr '\n' ' '`), so a "STOPPED BEFORE ... (cap, driver admission)" end line did not match exactly; the reset launcher has the same line but no reset scenario checked an exact remote end line. Guard: the state is trimmed (`sed 's/[[:space:]]*$//'`) and the churn harness scenario "the driver's cap admission stops the run between stages" anchors the end line with `$`, so a trailing space fails it (it failed in harness run 2, the negative test).
- 2026-11-12 (churn launcher review r1, Sol and Astra reject): (1) adapting a reviewed launcher, I added a driver input (PRIOR) without carrying it into the deadlines, and kept attempt times taken after creation returned; (2) the new cap scenario injected the final status text, so the harness never executed the generated remote script. Guard: the churn harness now executes the pushed remote script under a scratch root with a stub driver (7 classifications, the call order and flags) and checks the accounting against the attempts file (deadlines net of PRIOR, --prior-usd rounded up from request times, --started = request time). Written rule for briefs: a new code path in a launcher needs a harness check that executes it, not one that feeds its output.
- 2026-11-12: an inline bash heredoc that itself contained `<<'EOF'` ended early, and the shell ran the rest of the snippet (no damage: empty temp folders). Guard: an edit script with nested heredocs goes to a scratch file via the Write tool and runs from there.
- 2026-11-12 (churn analysis): I ran churn_block.py with --records pointing at the churn bundle's eval-all, which holds only the gate cell; the loader treated the 17 absent registered no-churn records as missing cells and wrote a partial result (no file committed). Guard: load() now refuses when any registered no-churn record is missing (test_load_refuses_a_missing_registered_no_churn_record, seen failing first); the registered records are results/v2/eval/all (the path in reset.json inputs).
- 2026-11-12 (churn results review r2, Sol and Astra caveats): my diagnostics validator compared shares with `abs(x - want) > TOL`, which a NaN passes, and checked lengths but not seed identifiers or bin labels. Guard: validate_diagnostics rejects non-finite shares, wrong or reordered age bins and non-unique or mismatched seeds (three tests, seen failing first). Rule for validators: test NaN, duplicates and a consistent relabelling, not only a changed value.
- 2026-11-13: I accepted a helper's report that the churn launcher's end-state parse returned the first marker, and reported it as a bug; a negative test showed the old parse was correct (only end states are capitalised after `== `). Guard: a reported defect is called a defect only after a test fails on it; reports from helpers and reviewers are hypotheses until then.
- 2026-11-13 (churn mid launcher, harness run 7): my defensive end-state parse edit put its comment before `; exit 1 ;;` on the same case line, so the comment swallowed the exit; on a stop or failure the launcher kept polling until the harness alarm (2 scenarios failed, exit 142). Guard: `results/pods/case_end_check.py` fails on any `;;` that only appears inside a trailing comment (negative test: the buggy line is refused); the churn harness runs it first and stops on a failure.
- 2026-11-13 (board): I wrote a guessed updatedAt (17:10Z) on job touchstone-runs-ab when the clock read 16:02Z; corrected at once. Guard: no board write without a `date -u` call in the same turn; the value is pasted from its output, never typed.

- 2026-11-13, Run B bundle: a one-line move-then-copy script went on after the move to the Trash failed (an empty
  target from a failed `mkdir`), so `cp` overwrote the old Mac gate records in place. Nothing was lost (copies are in
